 or confirmed whether he intends to enter the race. if he chooses to run, issues facing franklin—including economic development, housing, municipal services, infrastructure, and the city’s long-term growth—would likely shape the discussion surrounding a potential candidacy. these same topics dominated candidate forums and voter conversations during the 2025 election cycle, when turnout rose noticeably compared with the prior municipal vote. the expressions of support for minnich remain informal, and no petition, campaign committee, or organized political group has been announced. the next formal steps would include minnich deciding whether to become a candidate and completing any filing requirements established for franklin’s 2027 municipal election. filing periods for city offices in new hampshire cities generally open in the summer of the election year, with exact dates set by the city clerk. franklin, a city of roughly 8,850 residents in merrimack county, operates under a mayor–city manager form of government. the mayor works with a nine-member city council (three wards) and appoints or serves on various boards. local leaders and residents continue to focus on balancing growth with the preservation of franklin’s character as a small lakes region community. more information about minnich’s professional background is available on his [linkedin profile](https://www.linkedin.com/in/bradleyminnich/). new-hampshire new-hampshire new hampshire nh franklin franklin, nh franklin mayor local government"
