      "id": "ma-maternal-mental-health-expansion-2026-09-15",
      "text": "massachusetts proposes expanded postpartum mental-health support gov. maura healey is seeking $2.25 million to broaden newborn home visits and strengthen a clinician-consultation program, alongside planned screening-rule changes. massachusetts gov. maura healey proposed new spending and regulatory changes tuesday aimed at identifying postpartum mental-health concerns earlier and connecting more families with care. healey plans to seek $2 million in a supplemental budget to expand the department of public health’s welcome family program statewide. the program offers parents and caregivers a free, one-time home visit from a maternal and child health nurse during the first weeks after a birth. the governor’s office says welcome family currently serves about 3,000 births a year, compared with roughly 68,000 births statewide. the proposed appropriation would make a visit available to every parent or caregiver, though lawmakers must approve the funding. a separate $250,000 proposal would strengthen the massachusetts child psychiatry access program for moms, which helps clinicians address perinatal mental-health needs. the department of public health also plans to revise regulations governing screening for mood and anxiety disorders during and after pregnancy. massachusetts massachusetts massachusetts ma boston boston, massachusetts massachusetts maternal health postpartum care mental health"
