      "text": "mattapan residents question center-running blue hill avenue bus lanes a boston and mbta proposal would place buses in the median, prompting questions about street crossings, businesses and other transit options. hundreds of mattapan residents packed a listening session thursday to raise concerns about a proposed redesign of blue hill avenue. the boston and mbta plan would put dedicated bus lanes in the center of the street, with riders crossing general traffic lanes to reach median boarding areas. opponents at morning star baptist church focused on pedestrian safety, access to neighborhood businesses and the effect of construction on the corridor. some speakers asked officials to consider alternatives, including a long-discussed extension of the orange line, rather than commit to the center-running layout. mayor michelle wu and mbta general manager phillip eng attended the session. wu said the design remains in progress and can change as public feedback is collected. the meeting was the first of four community sessions planned over the next several weeks, leaving additional opportunities for residents to review details and respond. the proposal aims to make bus trips more reliable on one of boston’s busiest transit corridors. the unresolved issue is how to balance that goal with safe crossings, traffic flow and commercial access. further design work will determine whether the city modifies station locations, lane arrangements or other street features before moving ahead. massachusetts massachusetts massachusetts massachusetts boston mattapan, massachusetts blue hill avenue mbta mattapan bus lanes"
    },
    "ma-mbta-federal-safety-oversight-2026-09-20": {
      "excerpt": "The Federal Transit Administration closed its safety management inspection and transferred direct oversight of the Massachusetts system back to the state Department of Public Utilities.",
      "id": "ma-mbta-federal-safety-oversight-2026-09-20",
      "text": "federal safety oversight of mbta ends after years-long review the federal transit administration closed its safety management inspection and transferred direct oversight of the massachusetts system back to the state department of public utilities. the federal transit administration has closed its safety management inspection of the massachusetts department of public utilities' oversight of the mbta, state officials said friday. the move transfers direct federal oversight that began in 2022 back to the dpu. the inspection followed years of federal concern about the transit system's safety practices. the healey administration and mbta said the closure reflects progress on corrective action plans, workforce changes and infrastructure investment, while acknowledging that the system still has work ahead. dpu commissioner staci rubin said the agency had strengthened its rail-safety staff, technical expertise and oversight processes. mbta general manager phillip eng said 99% of the system's corrective action plans had been submitted, according to the state and the transit authority. the mbta remains under state oversight as it continues track, signal, vehicle and accessibility projects across the region. the federal action marks a change in supervision, not the end of safety monitoring. massachusetts massachusetts massachusetts ma boston boston and eastern massachusetts massachusetts mbta public transit transportation safety"
    },
