      "text": "providence mayoral primary turns on rent control and housing growth mayor brett smiley and state rep. david morales are offering competing answers to the city’s affordability crisis ahead of wednesday’s democratic primary. providence voters are weighing sharply different housing strategies in wednesday’s democratic mayoral primary, where affordability has become the central issue, the wall street journal reported. incumbent mayor brett smiley is emphasizing new housing construction and growth. state rep. david morales, his challenger, is advocating rent control as a more immediate response for tenants facing high rents. the argument reflects a tension familiar across new england: additional supply may take years to affect prices, while stronger rent protections can change the economics developers consider when deciding whether to build. both campaigns are presenting their approach as a response to the same shortage and cost pressure. the primary will give providence voters a direct choice over whether city government should prioritize faster production, stronger tenant protections or a combination of both. the result could shape the city’s housing debate and influence the november campaign. that debate is especially consequential in a city where housing policy is a daily cost-of-living issue rather than an abstract planning question. new-england rhode-island rhode island ri providence providence, ri rhode island providence housing mayor"
    },
