    "ri-blaze-orange-hunting-season-september-2026": {
      "excerpt": "Visitors to state management areas and undeveloped state parks must wear at least 200 square inches of fluorescent orange from September 12 through February 28.",
      "id": "ri-blaze-orange-hunting-season-september-2026",
      "text": "rhode island blaze-orange rule begins as archery season opens visitors to state management areas and undeveloped state parks must wear at least 200 square inches of fluorescent orange from september 12 through february 28. rhode island’s seasonal fluorescent-orange requirement takes effect saturday, september 12, as archery hunting gets underway on public lands. the department of environmental management says the rule applies through february 28 to people entering state management areas and undeveloped state parks during hunting season. visitors must wear at least 200 square inches of solid fluorescent orange, an amount that can generally be met with an orange hat or vest. the requirement is intended to make hikers, dog walkers, birders and other outdoor users more visible to hunters sharing the same properties. archery hunters are exempt from the orange requirement while participating in archery deer season, according to dem. other rules can vary by activity and season, so hunters and recreational visitors should review current regulations before entering public land. the agency’s reminder is especially relevant during the early fall overlap between popular hiking weather and expanding hunting activity. bright clothing, awareness of posted notices and keeping pets under close control can reduce avoidable conflicts. dem also advises visitors to know the boundaries and allowed uses of the property they plan to enter. rhode-island rhode-island rhode island ri rhode island hunting season blaze orange rhode island dem outdoor safety"
    },
    "ri-blood-center-childhood-cancer-donors-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The center says many young cancer patients depend on blood components and is promoting a September 14 community drive in Warwick.",
      "id": "ri-blood-center-childhood-cancer-donors-2026",
      "text": "rhode island blood center urges donors during childhood cancer campaign the center says many young cancer patients depend on blood components and is promoting a september 14 community drive in warwick. the rhode island blood center is using childhood cancer awareness month to remind residents that transfusions are a routine and often urgent part of treatment for many young cancer patients. more than 15,000 children and adolescents are diagnosed with cancer in the united states each year, the center said. chemotherapy, surgery and complications from treatment can create a need for red cells, platelets or plasma. the center estimates that more than 60% of people are eligible to donate blood, while only about 3% do so. nbc 10 highlighted the family of carrigan nelson, who was diagnosed with osteosarcoma shortly after her 18th birthday. her family said blood products helped extend her life during seven years of treatment before her death in 2025. they are encouraging eligible donors to give in her memory and for other patients. a community back-to-school blood drive is scheduled for monday, september 14, from 8 a.m. to 7 p.m. at the crowne plaza in warwick. donor eligibility and appointment details are available from the rhode island blood center. rhode-island rhode-island rhode island ri warwick warwick, ri blood donation childhood cancer warwick rhode island blood center"
    },
