    "ri-child-advocate-child-protection-system-report-2026": {
      "excerpt": "A review of 30 fatalities and 11 near-fatalities identified inconsistent screening, high caseloads and missed opportunities to connect families with help.",
      "id": "ri-child-advocate-child-protection-system-report-2026",
      "text": "rhode island child advocate calls for immediate child-protection changes a review of 30 fatalities and 11 near-fatalities identified inconsistent screening, high caseloads and missed opportunities to connect families with help. rhode island’s office of the child advocate says the state’s child-protection system needs immediate changes after a review identified persistent weaknesses in screening, staffing and prevention services. the review covered 30 fatalities and 11 near-fatalities involving children with current or prior department of children, youth and families contact. child advocate katelyn medeiros said reviewers found cases that should have been accepted for investigation but were screened out, along with families who sought help but were not connected to prevention services. the report contains 76 recommendations spanning child protective services practices, the support and response unit, oversight and longer-term system improvements. investigators also found caseloads above national best-practice levels and delays in responding to families within the department’s own timelines. medeiros said staffing is part of the problem but not the only answer; policy, law, regulation and internal systems also need better alignment. the findings describe a reform agenda rather than completed changes. accountability will depend on which recommendations dcyf accepts, the timeline for implementation and whether lawmakers or administrators provide the necessary authority and resources. rhode-island rhode-island rhode island ri providence providence, rhode island dcyf child welfare rhode island office of the child advocate"
    },
    "ri-clinical-research-network-26-million-grant-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The five-year NIH award will fund a third phase of Advance RI-CTR, expanding research support, partnerships and community engagement.",
      "id": "ri-clinical-research-network-26-million-grant-2026",
      "text": "$26 million grant renews statewide rhode island clinical-research network the five-year nih award will fund a third phase of advance ri-ctr, expanding research support, partnerships and community engagement. the national institutes of health has awarded $26 million to renew rhode island's statewide clinical and translational research hub for another five years. brown university hosts advance ri-ctr, which links academic institutions, health systems and community organizations to move discoveries from laboratories toward clinical and community use. the third phase will provide researchers with consultation in quantitative and qualitative methods, implementation science, artificial intelligence and digital health. it will also broaden regional and national partnerships, offer multiple funding mechanisms for emerging research leaders and expand access to data and other research resources. participating institutions include brown, the university of rhode island, brown university health, care new england and the providence va system. since 2016, the program says $11.2 million awarded to rhode island investigators has helped them secure another $132.4 million in public and private funding. it has supported 141 investigators, contributed to six commercial patents and trained more than 340 research mentors. the renewal is intended to deepen that infrastructure while giving community priorities a larger role in deciding which health questions move from research into practice. rhode-island rhode-island rhode island rhode island providence rhode island nih brown university advance ri-ctr medical research rhode island"
    },
