    "ri-southern-new-england-foliage-delay-forecast-2026": {
      "excerpt": "Forecasters say above-average warmth may push southern New England’s best color back one or two weeks, while calm conditions could help leaves stay on trees.",
      "id": "ri-southern-new-england-foliage-delay-forecast-2026",
      "text": "warmer season could delay rhode island peak foliage into november forecasters say above-average warmth may push southern new england’s best color back one or two weeks, while calm conditions could help leaves stay on trees. rhode island’s peak fall color may arrive later than usual this year, with a warmer growing season potentially pushing the best viewing into early november. storm team 10 says the typical late-october peak across rhode island, southeastern massachusetts and southern connecticut could be delayed by one to two weeks. the outlook is a forecast, not a fixed calendar. leaf color and timing depend on temperatures, daylight, moisture and wind, and conditions can vary between inland and coastal locations. above-average warmth can slow the seasonal changes that trigger leaves to turn, while a major windstorm can shorten the viewing window by stripping leaves early. forecasters said the region’s relatively calm summer and early fall may help leaves remain on branches. they also pointed to adequate precipitation after a dry spring as a factor that could support healthy color. despite the possible delay, the forecast continues to call for vibrant foliage. for visitors planning trips, the timing means flexibility may be more useful than relying on a single traditional peak weekend. northern new england usually turns first, followed by central and southern areas. residents should watch updated local foliage maps and short-range weather forecasts as october approaches, since a cold snap or strong coastal storm could still change the schedule. rhode-island rhode-island rhode island ri rhode island rhode island fall foliage weather southern new england"
    },
    "ri-spotted-lanternfly-vineyards-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The invasive insect is established in all five counties, prompting vineyards and state researchers to expand monitoring of vines and nearby host trees.",
      "id": "ri-spotted-lanternfly-vineyards-2026",
      "text": "spotted lanternfly spread puts rhode island vineyards on watch the invasive insect is established in all five counties, prompting vineyards and state researchers to expand monitoring of vines and nearby host trees. rhode island vineyards are intensifying surveillance for spotted lanternflies as the invasive insect becomes established across all five counties. the rhode island department of environmental management says the species was first detected in warwick in 2021, with the state’s first confirmed population identified near route 7 in smithfield the following year. growers told nbc 10 they are working with ridem and university of rhode island researchers who regularly check monitoring traps. spotted lanternflies feed on sap from grapevines and other plants. large infestations can weaken vines, reduce yields and leave plants more vulnerable to cold. their sugary waste can also encourage sooty mold. tree-of-heaven is a preferred host, so its presence near vineyards is one factor growers watch. researchers are studying possible controls, including a parasitoid that attacks lanternfly nymphs, but no biological-control agent is currently approved for release in rhode island. for now, early detection, reporting and removal of egg masses remain central tools. residents should use ridem guidance before handling suspected insects or moving outdoor materials that may carry eggs. new-england rhode-island rhode island ri rhode island spotted lanternfly vineyards ridem university of rhode island"
    },
