    "ri-tiverton-pocasset-ridge-conservation-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The state and The Nature Conservancy protected forestland that connects three existing open-space areas in eastern Rhode Island.",
      "id": "ri-tiverton-pocasset-ridge-conservation-2026",
      "text": "tiverton conservation deal links 27 acres to pocasset ridge the state and the nature conservancy protected forestland that connects three existing open-space areas in eastern rhode island. rhode island environmental officials and the nature conservancy have completed a 27-acre conservation deal in tiverton, adding forestland to the pocasset ridge conservation area and strengthening a connected corridor of protected open space. the nature conservancy paid $400,000 for the property. the rhode island department of environmental management acquired a conservation easement using $225,000 from the federal forest legacy program, according to the agencies’ september 8 announcement. the parcel links pocasset ridge with the pardon gray preserve and weetamoo woods. officials said the broader partnership has conserved about 1,600 contiguous acres over 25 years and that roughly 80% of the ridge is now protected. the land includes coastal oak-holly forest, a globally rare habitat that supports wildlife and helps protect local headwaters. the easement limits development while keeping the property within the larger conservation network. for tiverton, the agreement closes another gap in a long-running effort to preserve one of the town’s major forested landscapes. rhode-island rhode-island rhode island ri tiverton tiverton, ri tiverton conservation pocasset ridge forest"
    },
    "ri-trash-truck-battery-fire-2026": {
      "excerpt": "Firefighters used a thermal camera to locate a lithium-ion battery after a Waste Management driver dumped the smoking load away from homes.",
      "id": "ri-trash-truck-battery-fire-2026",
      "text": "battery pack blamed for north smithfield trash-truck fire firefighters used a thermal camera to locate a lithium-ion battery after a waste management driver dumped the smoking load away from homes. a discarded lithium-ion battery pack is believed to have started a fire inside a waste management trash truck in north smithfield on tuesday, local officials said. the truck began smoking on second avenue. the driver moved it away from the residential area to a transfer station at 14 canal street, then emptied the burning load beside the road so firefighters could reach the material. crews extinguished the fire and used a thermal camera to identify a battery pack as the likely source. a woonsocket hazardous-materials team contained and disposed of the battery, while waste management handled cleanup of the remaining debris. no injuries were reported. the incident illustrates why rechargeable batteries should not go into household trash or curbside recycling. when crushed or punctured during collection and compaction, the cells can enter thermal runaway and ignite surrounding material. residents should instead use a designated battery drop-off or household hazardous-waste program and cover exposed terminals when local guidance calls for it. the quick decision to move the truck away from homes limited the immediate risk, but the load still required specialized handling. new-england ri north smithfield, rhode island north smithfield lithium-ion batteries trash truck fire safety"
    },
