      "id": "ri-uri-oceanography-science-saturday-2026-2026",
      "text": "uri opens ocean labs for free science saturday the september 26 event at the narragansett bay campus will feature robotics tours, live deep-sea exploration and dozens of hands-on exhibits. the university of rhode island’s graduate school of oceanography will open its narragansett bay campus to the public for science saturday on september 26. the free family event runs from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. at 215 south ferry road and will bring together coastal scientists, engineers, educators and students for tours and hands-on demonstrations. dozens of exhibits will cover deep-sea life, rhode island’s blue economy, ocean acidification, climate adaptation and underwater sound. visitors can tour the ocean robotics laboratory and its large test tank, watch live pacific ocean exploration through the inner space center, see local marine life at the ann gall durbin aquarium and examine sediment cores kept in the marine geological samples laboratory. small research boats will be displayed, and guests can try on a survival suit or join volcanic-eruption demonstrations and crafts. uri says the event is certified as a green event by the rhode island department of environmental management. registration is suggested but not required, making the program a broad public showcase for the research facilities and ocean industries concentrated along narragansett bay. rhode-island rhode-island rhode island rhode island narragansett narragansett, rhode island university of rhode island oceanography science saturday narragansett ocean robotics"
    },
    "ri-uri-surf-therapy-visual-impairment-study-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The free five-week pilot will examine physical skills and perceived self-competence in participants ages 5 to 17.",
      "id": "ri-uri-surf-therapy-visual-impairment-study-2026",
      "text": "uri surf-therapy study recruits youth with visual impairments the free five-week pilot will examine physical skills and perceived self-competence in participants ages 5 to 17. a university of rhode island surf-therapy program is recruiting children and teenagers with blindness or visual impairments for a new pilot study at narragansett town beach, the university announced wednesday. the free five-week program is open to participants ages 5 to 17 and will run on saturdays from september 12 through october 10. no swimming experience is required, individualized support will be provided, and wetsuits and safety equipment will be available. participants will complete adapted surf sessions, a physical movement and activity assessment using auditory and tactile supports, questionnaires and a reflection on the overall experience. researchers will examine how the program may affect physical skills and participants’ perceived self-competence. uri associate professor emily clapham started the broader surf-therapy program in 2010 for youth with a range of disabilities. it served about 50 children ages 3 to 21 last year. the new expansion is part of graduate student wendy flanagan’s master’s project and is supported by a grant from the john e. fogarty foundation. organizers are also seeking volunteers for the fall sessions. rhode-island rhode-island rhode island ri narragansett narragansett, ri uri surf therapy visual impairment adaptive recreation"
    },
    "ri-uri-technology-showcase-startup-week-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The September 22 event will put university inventions and startups in front of investors and industry partners during Rhode Island Startup Week.",
      "id": "ri-uri-technology-showcase-startup-week-2026",
      "text": "uri technology showcase will connect research with investors the september 22 event will put university inventions and startups in front of investors and industry partners during rhode island startup week. the university of rhode island will hold its second annual technology showcase on september 22, bringing university researchers, startup founders, investors and industry representatives together in kingston. the event is scheduled for 4 to 7 p.m. at toray commons in the fascitelli center for advanced engineering. it is part of rhode island startup week and is designed to move research toward licensing, partnerships and new ventures. uri said featured work will include smart-wound monitoring and pfas detection. the university also pointed to recent commercialization activity: spinout juice robotics has licensed four uri technologies, trace sensing closed a seed round of more than $2 million and fastflowlm joined an amd program. those examples do not guarantee that every project at the showcase will attract funding or reach the market. they illustrate the event’s purpose: giving potential backers and partners an early look at applied research. registration and the final program are available through uri, and schedule details may change before september 22. rhode-island rhode-island rhode island ri kingston kingston, rhode island university of rhode island technology transfer startups research"
