      "excerpt": "ASML’s next-generation lithography tools are drawing commitments from major chipmakers even as the machines cost roughly twice as much as standard EUV systems.",
      "id": "tech-asml-high-na-chipmaking-demand-2026-09-14",
      "text": "asml extends chipmaking lead as customers embrace high na asml’s next-generation lithography tools are drawing commitments from major chipmakers even as the machines cost roughly twice as much as standard euv systems. asml’s newest high-numerical-aperture extreme-ultraviolet machines are becoming a larger part of chipmakers’ plans, reuters reported monday. the dutch company’s standard euv systems, which cost about $200 million, were described as sold out through 2027. tsmc, samsung, sk hynix and intel have committed roughly $400 million to high na tools, according to the report. the machines can print features about 40% smaller than the previous generation, but their price is roughly twice as high. intel has processed more than one million wafers on its high na system. the equipment matters because smaller features can support denser and more powerful chips, but adoption also raises the cost of building advanced manufacturing capacity. asml remains the key supplier of euv lithography equipment, giving its delivery schedule and customer commitments importance across the semiconductor industry. the transition also places pressure on chipmakers to justify more expensive tools as they race to secure capacity for advanced processors. tech tech tech netherlands veldhoven veldhoven, netherlands asml semiconductors euv high na"
    },
    "tech-asml-large-mask-high-na-euv-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The chip-equipment maker says an industry consortium could adapt its most advanced lithography systems for today’s largest AI processors.",
      "id": "tech-asml-large-mask-high-na-euv-2026",
      "text": "asml plans larger masks for high-na tools and data-center chips the chip-equipment maker says an industry consortium could adapt its most advanced lithography systems for today’s largest ai processors. asml plans to work with major chipmakers on larger masks for its newest high-na extreme-ultraviolet lithography tools, aiming to make the systems suitable for large data-center processors. reuters reported that widely used euv machines can print chips up to about 800 square millimeters. high-na tools can create finer features, but their smaller masks currently limit the size of chips they can produce. asml said a larger mask format could remove that constraint and expand adoption beyond intel, which already uses the new tools for some production. tsmc plans high-na use in advanced-node high-volume manufacturing from 2030, while samsung and sk hynix are targeting memory production earlier. the timetable is long: asml expects a pilot line around 2031 and technology ready for high-volume production by 2033. chief technology officer marco pieters told reuters the change could raise system productivity by 40%. those are company plans, not guaranteed manufacturing outcomes, and success will require equipment makers, chip foundries and mask suppliers to align on a new standard. tech nl netherlands asml semiconductors euv ai chips"
    },
    "tech-australia-algorithm-opt-out-2026": {
      "excerpt": "Draft legislation would require social platforms to give users 16 and older a lasting choice between recommended content and posts from accounts they follow.",
      "id": "tech-australia-algorithm-opt-out-2026",
      "text": "australia proposes right to opt out of algorithmic feeds draft legislation would require social platforms to give users 16 and older a lasting choice between recommended content and posts from accounts they follow. australia’s government has proposed legislation that would require social-media platforms to let users age 16 and older opt out of algorithmically recommended feeds. under the plan, services would have to notify eligible users and provide a genuine, enduring choice between recommendation-driven content and a feed centered on friends or creators they follow. the proposal extends australia’s scrutiny of platform design beyond its existing ban on social-media accounts for children under 16. a broader digital duty of care would also apply to services including games, apps and artificial-intelligence chatbots. those companies would be expected to protect children from features regulators consider addictive or damaging to self-esteem. the maximum penalty described for a breach is 109.2 million australian dollars, equivalent to about $78.6 million in the associated press report. the measures are proposed laws and have not yet taken effect. their practical impact will depend on the final text, parliamentary approval and enforcement rules. if enacted, the opt-out requirement could test whether large platforms can provide a truly persistent non-algorithmic experience rather than a temporary setting that quietly resets. tech international canberra, australia australia social media algorithms online safety"
    },
    "tech-australia-telco-resilience-optus-outage-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The government’s response follows a 76-minute disruption that affected voice calls and some emergency-service connections across several regions.",
      "id": "tech-australia-telco-resilience-optus-outage-2026",
      "text": "australia pledges stronger telecom resilience after optus outage the government’s response follows a 76-minute disruption that affected voice calls and some emergency-service connections across several regions. australia’s government pledged sunday to strengthen the resilience of national telecommunications networks after an optus outage disrupted voice service and some emergency calls. the 76-minute technical problem affected customers in victoria, south australia, tasmania and the northern territory, according to the country’s second-largest carrier. optus described the customer impact as intermittent call dropouts. the government response raises the issue beyond one company’s technical repair, focusing attention on how carriers maintain access to police, fire and ambulance services during network failures. officials had not yet detailed a specific regulatory package or timetable for new requirements. the disruption follows a separate optus network failure last year that prevented hundreds of people from reaching emergency services and was linked to two deaths. that history increases scrutiny of redundancy, incident response and customer communication across the sector. the latest pledge signals that canberra expects resilience work to cover the broader telecom system, not only optus’s immediate outage review. tech tech tech new south wales sydney sydney, australia optus australia telecommunications emergency calls"
    },
    "tech-automattic-mullenweg-returns-ceo-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The WordPress.com parent said its founder again has the board’s support days after directors placed him on paid leave.",
      "id": "tech-automattic-mullenweg-returns-ceo-2026",
      "text": "automattic confirms matt mullenweg has returned as ceo the wordpress.com parent said its founder again has the board’s support days after directors placed him on paid leave. automattic confirmed saturday that wordpress co-founder matt mullenweg has returned as chairman and chief executive after a board vote placed him on paid leave earlier in the week. a company spokesperson told techcrunch that mullenweg had the board’s full support, reversing the leadership arrangement announced days earlier. automattic had said wednesday that chief financial officer mark davies would serve as interim ceo while mullenweg was on leave. techcrunch reported that mullenweg resisted the move and told employees he was back in control. the company’s latest statement formally resolves the chief executive question for now, though the reasons for the original leave remain undisclosed. questions also remain about automattic’s board composition. techcrunch said it had sought comment on reports that board member and former automattic ceo toni schneider stepped down, but had not received confirmation. the episode creates an unusual governance moment for the company behind wordpress.com and other publishing, commerce and messaging products. tech tech tech california san francisco san francisco, california automattic matt mullenweg wordpress technology leadership"
    },
    "tech-bitcoin-suisse-swiss-job-cuts-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The crypto-services company says it may eliminate 60 roles while shifting software development and back-office work to international hubs.",
      "id": "tech-bitcoin-suisse-swiss-job-cuts-2026",
      "text": "bitcoin suisse plans to cut up to half its swiss workforce the crypto-services company says it may eliminate 60 roles while shifting software development and back-office work to international hubs. cryptocurrency services company bitcoin suisse plans to eliminate as many as 60 of its 120 jobs in switzerland, reuters reported. the zug-based firm is consulting employees through september 20 before finalizing the reductions, which would amount to as much as half of its domestic workforce. the reorganization is intended to consolidate software development and back-office operations in international hubs, where the company says it can scale faster, reach a broader pool of workers and direct investment toward future services. bitcoin suisse will also close its information-technology development site in copenhagen. the changes show how crypto companies are reassessing where they build and support increasingly software-intensive financial products. founded in 2013, bitcoin suisse employs about 200 people worldwide. beyond switzerland, it has operations in liechtenstein, abu dhabi and bermuda, as well as an international hub in bratislava and another planned for vietnam. the consultation means the maximum job-cut figure is not yet final. the company did not frame the announcement as an exit from switzerland, where its headquarters and core crypto-finance business remain based. tech tech tech switzerland zug zug, switzerland bitcoin suisse cryptocurrency software development job cuts switzerland"
    },
    "tech-boards-digital-infrastructure-resilience-capgemini-2026": {
      "excerpt": "A survey of 1,300 organizations found that replacing a critical provider could take months or longer, pushing resilience into board discussions.",
      "id": "tech-boards-digital-infrastructure-resilience-capgemini-2026",
      "text": "boards elevate digital-infrastructure resilience as risks grow a survey of 1,300 organizations found that replacing a critical provider could take months or longer, pushing resilience into board discussions. large companies and public bodies are putting critical digital infrastructure under more direct board-level scrutiny as cyber threats, export controls and geopolitical conflict expose technology dependencies, a capgemini survey found. the survey covered executives at 1,300 large organizations in 11 countries, reuters reported. respondents increasingly treated cloud services, data, artificial-intelligence models and other digital systems like energy supplies or physical supply chains that require risk assessments and contingency plans. nearly half said replacing a critical provider would take between three months and a year. more than a third expected the switch to take longer than a year, suggesting that contractual access alone does not guarantee a quick recovery when a major vendor becomes unavailable. the response is not necessarily a wholesale shift away from established providers. organizations are focusing on whether important workloads can move, whether proprietary assets remain under their control and whether alternate suppliers are practical. that emphasis turns technical resilience into a governance and business-continuity issue, not solely an information-technology concern. tech tech tech international global global digital infrastructure cybersecurity capgemini business continuity"
    },
    "tech-boeing-engineers-tentative-contract-speea-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The revised agreement would deliver a 10% initial raise and annual increases through 2030 as key certification programs remain delayed.",
      "id": "tech-boeing-engineers-tentative-contract-speea-2026",
      "text": "boeing engineers reach tentative four-year contract the revised agreement would deliver a 10% initial raise and annual increases through 2030 as key certification programs remain delayed. boeing and the society of professional engineering employees in aerospace have reached a tentative four-year contract after union members overwhelmingly rejected an earlier offer in august. the revised proposal would provide a 10% general wage increase on october 16, followed by 4% annual increases plus merit raises through 2030. the agreement also revises work-from-home rules and overtime limits. union officials said members had pressed for larger across-the-board increases after seattle-area inflation rose 4.5% over the previous year. speea’s negotiating team said the package did not satisfy every demand but represented a meaningful improvement over boeing’s prior proposal. members must still ratify the deal before the current contract expires on october 6. the vote matters beyond compensation: boeing engineers are central to certification work for the delayed 737 max 10 and 777-9 aircraft. a rejection could revive the possibility of a strike at a time when both programs face schedule pressure. the tentative settlement is the first fully negotiated contract between the sides since 2012, after extensions in 2016 and 2020. tech tech tech washington seattle seattle, washington boeing speea aerospace engineering labor aircraft certification"
    },
    "tech-boston-logan-amelia-ai-holograms-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The airport’s new “Amelia” assistants offer directions in every terminal and can send step-by-step routes to travelers’ phones by QR code.",
      "id": "tech-boston-logan-amelia-ai-holograms-2026",
      "text": "boston logan deploys eight multilingual ai holograms the airport’s new “amelia” assistants offer directions in every terminal and can send step-by-step routes to travelers’ phones by qr code. boston logan international airport has installed eight artificial-intelligence-powered holograms to help travelers find gates, restaurants, baggage claim and other locations. the assistants, named “amelia” after aviation pioneer amelia earhart, are positioned across every terminal, both before and after security checkpoints. the system can provide step-by-step directions in several languages and display a qr code so travelers can carry those instructions on their phones. airport officials said amelia can be programmed with thousands of locations and described the deployment as the largest use of this hologram technology at any airport. logan expects the units to handle about 100,000 interactions during their first year. massport says the goal is to supplement its customer-service staff, not replace employees. the current version is focused on common wayfinding questions, while a planned second phase would make the system more conversational and capable of answering a broader range of requests. the installation gives logan a visible test of how generative ai and projection technology can be integrated into busy public spaces. tech tech tech ma boston boston, massachusetts boston logan airport artificial intelligence amelia massport travel technology"
    },
    "tech-california-child-social-media-ai-laws-2026": {
      "excerpt": "A 13-bill package restricts addictive platform features for users under 16, bars AI companion toys for four years and expands protections against synthetic abuse material.",
      "id": "tech-california-child-social-media-ai-laws-2026",
      "text": "california enacts new child safeguards for social media and ai toys a 13-bill package restricts addictive platform features for users under 16, bars ai companion toys for four years and expands protections against synthetic abuse material. california governor gavin newsom signed a package of 13 bills imposing new safeguards on social media platforms, artificial-intelligence chatbots and technology products used by children. one measure bars platforms from exposing users under 16 to engagement features defined as psychologically exploitative, including infinite-scroll feeds and algorithmic autoplay. another prohibits the manufacture and sale of toys containing ai companion chatbots for four years. related provisions require parental controls and safety assessments for chatbot programs. the package also expands criminal sanctions for child sexual abuse material to cover digitally altered or ai-generated depictions of minors. newsom described the measures as the country’s broadest youth-technology protections, while supporters in both parties said existing safeguards had not kept pace with product design. the electronic frontier foundation criticized the social-media restrictions as a threat to privacy and free expression, illustrating the legal debate likely to follow implementation. california joins states including utah, texas, florida and new york in regulating minors’ access to major platforms, but the details and enforcement models differ. agencies must still define portions of the rules before the requirements take full effect. tech tech tech ca sacramento california california social media ai child safety"
    },
    "tech-canal-laliga-antipiracy-pact-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The broadcasters will combine technical tools, investigations and policy work against illegal soccer streams in their shared markets.",
      "id": "tech-canal-laliga-antipiracy-pact-2026",
      "text": "canal+ and laliga expand anti-piracy partnership across nearly 50 countries the broadcasters will combine technical tools, investigations and policy work against illegal soccer streams in their shared markets. canal+ and spain’s laliga have signed an anti-piracy agreement covering nearly 50 countries where the media company carries league matches. reuters reported that the partners will combine technical capabilities, coordinate investigations and work together on policy aimed at illegal streaming networks. the territory spans europe, sub-saharan africa and haiti. financial terms were not disclosed. the arrangement extends beyond removing individual streams: both organizations described piracy as a cross-border business supported by increasingly sophisticated distribution systems. laliga said spanish clubs’ broadcast income fell 5.2% in the 2024-25 season to 1.43 billion euros, even as commercial revenue increased. canal+ estimated that piracy destroys about 1.5 billion euros of value annually in france, including losses to public tax revenue. the partnership matters technologically because rights holders increasingly need automated detection, platform cooperation and evidence collection that works across jurisdictions. illegal services can blend licensed and unlicensed programming and shift infrastructure quickly. sharing tools and case information could make enforcement faster, though success will still depend on local law, internet providers and courts in each market. tech tech tech france paris paris, france canal+ laliga streaming piracy sports media cybersecurity"
    },
