      "id": "tech-china-ai-chip-prices-hbm-shortage-2026",
      "text": "chinese ai chipmakers raise prices as memory shortage bites reuters sources say huawei, cambricon and smaller rivals are repricing accelerators as high-bandwidth memory costs rise. chinese artificial-intelligence chipmakers are raising indicated prices for current and next-generation processors as high-bandwidth memory becomes more expensive and harder to obtain, three people familiar with the discussions told reuters. huawei increased the indicated price of its ascend 950dt accelerator card to more than 250,000 yuan, about $37,255, according to two sources. that is 20% to 50% above quotes given two months earlier, depending on contract terms. huawei has said the card is scheduled for availability in the fourth quarter of 2026. cambricon raised the indicated price of a planned chip tentatively called the 690 by 20% to 30%, two sources said. smaller competitors metax and iluvatar corex have made similar adjustments. the sources were not identified because the pricing discussions are private. high-bandwidth memory is crucial for moving large quantities of data through ai accelerators. export restrictions and reliance on gray-market supplies have increased pressure on chinese producers seeking domestic alternatives to nvidia. the new quotes may raise deployment costs for buyers and complicate efforts to expand locally produced ai computing capacity. tech tech tech intl shanghai china ai chips huawei cambricon high-bandwidth memory"
    },
    "tech-china-autonomous-vehicle-roadmap-2030": {
      "excerpt": "A new industry roadmap calls for self-driving systems on highways, urban expressways and selected city roads while tightening safety and software rules.",
      "id": "tech-china-autonomous-vehicle-roadmap-2030",
      "text": "china targets large-scale autonomous-vehicle deployment by 2030 a new industry roadmap calls for self-driving systems on highways, urban expressways and selected city roads while tightening safety and software rules. china’s industry ministry has set a 2030 goal for large-scale deployment of autonomous vehicles, pairing expansion plans with tighter oversight of vehicle production, road safety and software updates. the roadmap calls for automated driving on highways, urban expressways and selected city streets. it also aims to produce multiple chinese automakers ranked among the world’s top 10 by sales and to increase the global influence of chinese brands and technical standards. safety is central to the plan: the ministry wants autonomous vehicles to achieve better safety performance than human drivers. regulators also intend to create special approval pathways for some components and low-volume vehicles while strengthening antitrust enforcement and product-quality supervision. the document addresses the industry’s capacity problem as well as its technology ambitions. officials plan to discourage overinvestment, eliminate inefficient production and encourage consolidation across automakers and battery manufacturers. china also wants deeper research and investment partnerships with foreign companies. the roadmap sets policy direction, but broad deployment will still depend on technical validation, local road approvals and enforcement of the new rules. tech tech tech china beijing china autonomous vehicles china electric vehicles transportation"
    },
