      "id": "tech-cognition-series-e-2026",
      "text": "cognition ai raises $2 billion at $48 billion valuation the autonomous coding-agent company says its run-rate revenue has climbed to nearly $900 million since a may financing. ai coding startup cognition ai said tuesday that it raised $2 billion in a series e financing that values the company at $48 billion, nearly double its valuation from a may funding round. reuters reported that new investors andreessen horowitz and accel led the round, with founders fund, general catalyst and avenir also participating. cognition said its run-rate revenue increased from $492 million to nearly $900 million since may, when it raised $1 billion at a $26 billion valuation. the company builds autonomous software-engineering agents designed to plan, write, test and deploy code with limited human intervention. the fresh capital gives cognition additional resources to develop those tools and compete in a crowded market for ai-assisted programming. the $48 billion figure is a private financing valuation, not a continuously traded public-market price. it reflects the terms accepted by investors in this round and may not translate directly into a future sale or public listing value. even so, the rapid step-up shows that large investors continue to place sizable bets on companies selling automation for software development. tech us united states cognition ai artificial intelligence coding agents venture capital"
    },
    "tech-covenant-anthem-mass-production-cruise-missile-2026": {
      "excerpt": "The startup says its Anthem missile has completed more than 200 test flights and is backed by about $150 million in orders.",
      "id": "tech-covenant-anthem-mass-production-cruise-missile-2026",
      "text": "covenant unveils lower-cost cruise missile designed for mass production the startup says its anthem missile has completed more than 200 test flights and is backed by about $150 million in orders. defense startup covenant has unveiled anthem, a ground-launched cruise missile the company says can be produced faster and at a lower price than many established long-range weapons. the company told reuters that anthem carries a payload exceeding 200 kilograms and has completed more than 200 test flights over the past year. covenant is targeting a mid-six-figure unit price once production reaches scale and says it has booked about $150 million in orders. founded in 2024, covenant has raised more than $250 million and employs over 200 people in the united states, germany and israel. its new dallas plant is designed for annual capacity of 5,000 missiles; a similarly sized german facility has the same target, while an israeli site is planned for 2,000. those figures are company plans, not completed production. new defense manufacturers often face certification, contracting and supply-chain obstacles when moving from tests to high-volume delivery. covenant expects an international customer to begin receiving german-built units late this year and u.s. army deliveries in 2027. tech tech tech tx dallas dallas, texas covenant anthem missile defense technology manufacturing"
    },
